1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,375
Chapter 43: Hemorrhagic Placental Disorders.

3
00:00:06,375 --> 00:00:19,400
Placental Abruption Diagnosis Management Placenta Previa Diagnosis Management Placenta Accreta Spectrum Diagnosis Management References Hemorrhage following partial or complete separation of the placenta can be torrential.

4
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:26,162
Recall that the amount of blood flowing through the intervillous space at term exceeds 600 mL/min .

5
00:00:26,162 --> 00:00:33,400
In the second half of pregnancy, three placental disorders contribute substantially to maternal mortality rates.

6
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:39,125
These include placental abruption, placenta previa, and placenta accreta spectrum.

7
00:00:39,125 --> 00:00:49,737
The contributions of hemorrhagic placental disorders to maternal mortality are discussed in Chapter 1 (p. 7) and Chapter 42 (p. 733).

8
00:00:49,737 --> 00:00:55,175
The management and clinical experience with these disorders span more than a generation.

9
00:00:55,175 --> 00:01:03,737
Placental abruption, for example, has been emphasized in this text for more than 50 years beginning with the work of Dr. Jack Pritchard.

10
00:01:03,737 --> 00:01:10,837
Now more common than years past, placenta accreta spectrum is another substantial threat to maternal well-being.

11
00:01:10,837 --> 00:01:12,925
Placental Abruption.

12
00:01:12,925 --> 00:01:15,862
# Etiopathogenesis.

13
00:01:15,862 --> 00:01:25,575
Separation of the placenta—either partially or totally—from its implantation site before delivery is called placental abruption or abruptio placentae.

14
00:01:25,575 --> 00:01:34,550
From Latin, the latter translates as “rending asunder of the placenta,” which denotes a sudden accident, which is characteristic of most cases.

15
00:01:34,550 --> 00:01:46,475
In the purest sense, the cumbersome—and thus seldom used—term premature separation of the normally implanted placenta is most descriptive because it excludes separation of a placenta previa.

16
00:01:46,475 --> 00:01:53,425
Abruption likely begins with rupture of a decidual spiral artery and hemorrhage into the decidual basalis.

17
00:01:53,425 --> 00:02:01,187
The subsequent expanding retroplacental hematoma splits the decidua and leaves a thin layer adherent to the myometrium.

18
00:02:01,187 --> 00:02:06,387
The decidual hematoma grows to lift away and compress the adjacent placenta.

19
00:02:06,387 --> 00:02:15,812
In some cases that are associated with preeclampsia, impaired trophoblastic invasion with subsequent atherosis is one underlying predisposition .

20
00:02:15,812 --> 00:02:19,850
Inflammation or infection also may be contributory .

21
00:02:19,850 --> 00:02:25,650
However, histological findings cannot be used to determine the timing of the abruption .

22
00:02:25,650 --> 00:02:30,975
In the early stages of placental abruption, clinical symptoms may be absent.

23
00:02:30,975 --> 00:02:38,075
Even with continued bleeding and placental separation, placental abruption can still be either total or partial .

24
00:02:38,075 --> 00:02:47,450
With either, bleeding typically insinuates itself between the membranes and uterine wall, ultimately escaping through the cervix to cause external hemorrhage.

25
00:02:47,450 --> 00:02:53,287
Less often, the blood is retained, leading to concealed hemorrhage and delayed diagnosis.

26
00:02:53,287 --> 00:02:57,525
The delay translates into greater maternal and fetal hazards.

27
00:02:57,525 --> 00:03:03,412
With concealed hemorrhage, the likelihood of consumptive coagulopathy is also increased.

28
00:03:03,412 --> 00:03:14,387
This is because increased pressure within the intervillous space, caused by the expanding retroplacental clot, forces more placental thromboplastin into the maternal circulation .

29
00:03:14,387 --> 00:03:16,962
Figure 43-1.

30
00:03:16,962 --> 00:03:19,837
Schematic of placental abruption.

31
00:03:19,837 --> 00:03:24,425
Shown to the left is total placental abruption with concealed hemorrhage.

32
00:03:24,425 --> 00:03:33,562
To the right is a partial abruption with blood and clots that dissect between membranes and decidua to reach the internal cervical os and then the vagina.

33
00:03:33,562 --> 00:03:39,725
Most blood in the retroplacental hematoma in a nontraumatic placental abruption is maternal.

34
00:03:39,725 --> 00:03:47,850
This is because hemorrhage derives from separation within the maternal decidua, and placental villi are usually initially intact.

35
00:03:47,850 --> 00:03:56,962
In 78 women at Parkland Hospital with a nontraumatic placental abruption, fetal-to-maternal hemorrhage was documented in only 20 percent.

36
00:03:56,962 --> 00:04:01,062
All of these had <10 mL fetal blood loss .

37
00:04:01,062 --> 00:04:09,025
In another series of 68 women with a placental abruption, fetal cells were found in peripheral blood in only 4 percent .

38
00:04:09,025 --> 00:04:16,300
Placental abruption can create a visible circumscribed depression on the maternal surface of a freshly delivered placenta.

39
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:22,975
These depressions usually measure a few centimeters in diameter and are covered by dark, clotted blood.

40
00:04:22,975 --> 00:04:29,625
Because several minutes are required for these anatomical changes to materialize, a very recently

41
00:04:29,788 --> 00:04:34,025
separated placenta may appear totally normal at delivery.

42
00:04:33,975 --> 00:04:43,288
Our experiences are like those of Benirschke and associates (2012) in that the “age” of the retroplacental clot cannot be determined exactly.

43
00:04:43,288 --> 00:04:49,150
In the example shown in Figure 43-2, a large dark clot is well formed.

44
00:04:49,150 --> 00:04:54,013
It has depressed the placental bulk and likely is at least several hours old.

45
00:04:54,013 --> 00:04:56,600
Figure 43-2.

46
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:00,413
Partial placental abruption with a dark adherent clot.

47
00:05:00,413 --> 00:05:04,538
Defining placental abruption severity is problematic.

48
00:05:04,538 --> 00:05:08,725
We consider placental abruption severe when the fetus dies.

49
00:05:08,725 --> 00:05:15,113
However, maternal and fetal complications are frequently serious even with a liveborn fetus.

50
00:05:15,113 --> 00:05:45,300
Ananth and coworkers (2016) have defined severe placental abruption as displaying one or more of the following: (1) maternal sequelae that include disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), shock, transfusion, hysterectomy, renal failure, or death; (2) fetal complications such as nonreassuring fetal status, growth restriction, or death; or (3) neonatal outcomes that include death, preterm delivery, or growth restriction.

51
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:53,738
Traumatic Abruption External trauma—usually from motor vehicle crashes or aggravated assault —can cause placental separation.

52
00:05:53,738 --> 00:05:57,825
The frequency of abruption originating from trauma varies.

53
00:05:57,825 --> 00:06:02,413
Importantly, abruption can stem from relatively minor trauma .

54
00:06:02,413 --> 00:06:08,825
The clinical presentation and consequences of these abruptions differ somewhat from spontaneous cases.

55
00:06:08,825 --> 00:06:23,850
For example, associated fetomaternal hemorrhage, while seldom clinically significant with most spontaneous abruptions, is more common with trauma because of concomitant placental tears or “fractures” (Fig. 50-10, p. 895).

56
00:06:23,850 --> 00:06:33,263
In eight women with traumatic placental abruption cared for at Parkland Hospital, we found fetal-to-maternal hemorrhage of 80 to 100 mL in three .

57
00:06:33,263 --> 00:06:42,650
Importantly, in some cases of trauma, a nonreassuring fetal heart rate tracing may not be accompanied by other evidence of placental separation.

58
00:06:42,650 --> 00:06:45,838
A sinusoidal tracing is one example.

59
00:06:45,838 --> 00:06:53,338
Following blunt abdominal trauma, uterine contractions are the single most important predictor of placental abruption .

60
00:06:53,338 --> 00:06:59,325
Traumatic abruption is considered in more detail in Chapter 50 (p. 894).

61
00:06:59,325 --> 00:07:06,525
Chronic Abruption When placental separation is not followed by delivery, the placental abruption is termed chronic.

62
00:07:06,525 --> 00:07:10,038
Some of these cases begin early in pregnancy.

63
00:07:10,038 --> 00:07:20,375
Dugoff and coworkers (2004) observed an association between some abnormally elevated maternal serum aneuploidy markers and subsequent abruption.

64
00:07:20,375 --> 00:07:29,825
Others have correlated first- and second-trimester bleeding with third-trimester placental abruption (Ananth, 2006; Weiss, 2004).

65
00:07:29,825 --> 00:07:39,050
In some cases, chronic abruption and oligohydramnios develop and are called the chronic abruption–oligohydramnios sequence (CAOS) .

66
00:07:39,050 --> 00:07:47,225
Even later in pregnancy, hemorrhage with retroplacental hematoma formation is occasionally arrested completely without delivery.

67
00:07:47,225 --> 00:08:00,850
These women may have abnormally elevated levels of maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (MSAFP) or placenta-specific RNAs as markers of the event (Miura, 2017; Ngai, 2012).

68
00:08:00,850 --> 00:08:03,113
# Frequency.

69
00:08:03,113 --> 00:08:09,775
The reported incidence of placental abruption varies because of differing criteria used for diagnosis.

70
00:08:09,775 --> 00:08:15,600
Its frequency averages 0.5 percent or 1 case in 200 deliveries.

71
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:26,075
From one database of more than 125 million births from 1979 through 2010, the incidence of placental abruption was nearly 1 percent .

72
00:08:26,075 --> 00:08:37,288
In more than 250,000 deliveries at Parkland Hospital from 2000 through 2015, the incidence of placental abruption averaged 1 case in 290 births .

73
00:08:37,288 --> 00:08:39,888
Figure 43-3.

74
00:08:39,888 --> 00:08:48,050
Frequency of placental abruption and placenta previa by maternal age at Parkland Hospital from 2000 through 2015.

75
00:08:48,050 --> 00:08:57,763
The frequency of placental abruption has risen in this country, and most of this increase is in black women (Ananth, 2005, 2016).

76
00:08:57,763 --> 00:09:05,625
This disproportion may be explained in part by the conservative management of early-onset preeclampsia in some institutions .

77
00:09:05,625 --> 00:09:11,800
At Parkland Hospital, however, the frequency of severe placental abruption has declined.

78
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:22,338
Specifically, with placental abruption so extensive as to kill the fetus, the incidence was 0.24 percent or 1 case in 420 births from

79
00:09:22,492 --> 00:09:26,154
1956 through 1967 .

80
00:09:26,104 --> 00:09:33,804
Through 2020, this same frequency dropped to 0.05 percent or 1 case in 2060 births.

81
00:09:33,804 --> 00:09:43,417
This likely reflects a concurrent decline in the number of high-parity women giving birth and rise in the availability of prenatal care and emergency transportation.

82
00:09:43,417 --> 00:09:46,929
# Perinatal Morbidity and Mortality.

83
00:09:46,929 --> 00:09:56,104
Overall, perinatal outcomes are influenced by gestational age, and the frequency of placental abruption rises across the third trimester.

84
00:09:56,104 --> 00:09:59,254
As seen in Figure 43-4.

85
00:09:59,254 --> 00:10:08,167
more than half of the placental abruptions at Parkland Hospital developed at gestational ages ≥37 weeks’ gestation.

86
00:10:08,167 --> 00:10:20,029
Perinatal mortality and morbidity, however, are more common with earlier abruptions and with concomitant preeclampsia (Furukawa, 2015; Han, 2019).

87
00:10:20,029 --> 00:10:29,579
Of other related factors, major fetal congenital anomalies have greater association with placental abruption (Riihimäki, 2013).

88
00:10:29,579 --> 00:10:32,279
Figure 43-4.

89
00:10:32,279 --> 00:10:37,279
Frequency of placental abruption by gestational age at Parkland Hospital.

90
00:10:37,279 --> 00:10:46,529
Although the rates of fetal death have declined, the contribution of abruption as a cause of stillbirth remains prominent because other causes also have decreased.

91
00:10:46,529 --> 00:10:56,929
For example, since the early 1990s, 10 to 12 percent of all third-trimester stillbirths at Parkland Hospital have been the consequence of placental abruption.

92
00:10:56,929 --> 00:11:11,404
In a review of more than 15 million singleton births between 1995 and 1998 in the United States, the perinatal mortality rate associated with placental abruption was 119 death per 1000 births.

93
00:11:11,404 --> 00:11:17,754
This was higher than the rate of 8 deaths per 1000 births in the general obstetrical population .

94
00:11:17,754 --> 00:11:22,167
Neonatal deaths also are common following placental abruption.

95
00:11:22,167 --> 00:11:26,867
At Parkland Hospital, 15 percent of liveborn neonates die.

96
00:11:26,867 --> 00:11:30,542
Perinatal morbidity also is common in this group .

97
00:11:30,542 --> 00:11:39,717
Moreover, associated childhood mortality mainly stems from birth-related asphyxia and prematurity (Riihimäki, 2018).

98
00:11:39,717 --> 00:11:51,179
In one series, 20 percent of 43 liveborn neonates had severe fetal acidemia following placental abruption, and 20 percent of survivors developed cerebral palsy .

99
00:11:51,179 --> 00:12:00,504
In another study of 84 liveborns, 29 (35 percent) had an umbilical artery pH <7.00 .

100
00:12:00,504 --> 00:12:08,379
Ananth and coworkers (2017) attribute some of the adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes to preterm delivery.

101
00:12:08,379 --> 00:12:11,342
# Predisposing Factors.

102
00:12:11,342 --> 00:12:17,079
Demographic Factors Several predisposing factors raise the placental abruption risk .

103
00:12:17,079 --> 00:12:25,529
Advancing maternal age is one risk, although data regarding women of great parity are conflicting (see Fig. 43-3) .

104
00:12:25,529 --> 00:12:28,992
Race or ethnicity also appears important .

105
00:12:28,992 --> 00:12:47,504
In an earlier study, in almost 366,000 deliveries at Parkland Hospital, abruption severe enough to kill the fetus was most common in black and white women—1 case in 200 births; less so in Asian women—1 in 300; and least common in Latinas—1 in 350 .

106
00:12:47,504 --> 00:12:52,817
A familial association was found from one Norwegian populationbased registry .

107
00:12:52,817 --> 00:12:58,442
In this study, if a woman had a severe abruption, the risk for her sister was doubled.

108
00:12:58,442 --> 00:13:08,742
Candidate genes involved in mitochondrial biogenesis and oxidative phosphorylation pathways, which confer risk for placental abruption, have been described .

109
00:13:08,742 --> 00:13:11,267
Table 43-1.

110
00:13:11,267 --> 00:13:21,029
Risk Factors for Placental Abruption Pregnancy-associated Hypertension Some form of hypertension is the most frequent condition associated with placental abruption.

111
00:13:21,029 --> 00:13:27,754
This includes gestational hypertension, preeclampsia, chronic hypertension, or a combination.

112
00:13:27,754 --> 00:13:37,204
The hemolysis, elevated liver enzyme levels, low platelet count (HELLP) syndrome carries an increased risk for placental abruption.

113
00:13:37,204 --> 00:13:46,292
Moreover, expectant management of preeclampsia in significantly preterm pregnancies was complicated by placental abruption in 4 percent .

114
00:13:46,292 --> 00:13:58,804
In a report by Pritchard and colleagues (1991) that described 408 women with placental abruption and fetal demise, hypertension was apparent in half once hypovolemia was corrected.

115
00:13:58,804 --> 00:14:04,529
Half of these latter women—a fourth of all 408—had chronic hypertension.

116
00:14:04,529 --> 00:14:16,229
Examined another way, one Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units (MFMU) Network study found that 1.5 percent of pregnant women with chronic hypertension suffered placental abruption .

117
00:14:16,229 --> 00:14:20,392
At Parkland Hospital, the placental abruption frequency

118
00:14:20,548 --> 00:14:26,523
in women with treated chronic hypertension and significant proteinuria was almost 1 percent.

119
00:14:26,473 --> 00:14:34,398
This incidence compared with the 0.3-percent incidence in women with treated hypertension without substantial proteinuria .

120
00:14:34,398 --> 00:14:41,510
Chronic hypertension with superimposed preeclampsia or with fetalgrowth restriction confers an even greater risk .

121
00:14:41,510 --> 00:14:52,010
Even so, the severity of hypertension does not necessarily correlate with abruption incidence (Morgan, 2016; Zetterstrom, 2005).

122
00:14:52,010 --> 00:15:02,885
However, women with preeclampsia that experience placental abruption have worse maternal, fetal, and neonatal outcomes compared with women experiencing abruption alone .

123
00:15:02,885 --> 00:15:18,210
The long-term effects of these associations are apparent from the significantly elevated cardiovascular mortality risk in women with prior abruption, with or without chronic hypertension (DeRoo, 2016; Pariente, 2013).

124
00:15:18,210 --> 00:15:30,298
Observations from the Magpie Trial Collaborative Group suggest that women with preeclampsia, with or without chronic hypertension, given magnesium sulfate may have a reduced risk for abruption .

125
00:15:30,298 --> 00:15:41,460
Last, Roberge and colleagues (2018), performed a metaanalysis and reported that a 100-mg daily aspirin may decrease the incidence of placental abruption.

126
00:15:41,460 --> 00:15:49,698
Preterm Prelabor Ruptured Membranes The placental abruption risk substantially rises when placental membranes rupture before term.

127
00:15:49,698 --> 00:15:58,473
Of 756 women with ruptured membranes between 20 and 36 weeks’ gestation, 5 percent developed an abruption .

128
00:15:58,473 --> 00:16:03,760
The frequency was 17 percent with previable prelabor ruptured membranes .

129
00:16:03,760 --> 00:16:09,948
The risk for abruption with preterm membrane rupture is further elevated with comorbid infection .

130
00:16:09,948 --> 00:16:20,198
In one cohort study of pregnancies ≥34 weeks’ gestation, the placental abruption rate was eightfold higher if hydramnios was comorbid .

131
00:16:20,198 --> 00:16:30,598
From a metaanalysis of 10 studies that included term and preterm gestational ages, hydramnios was associated with a twofold greater rate of placental abruption .

132
00:16:30,598 --> 00:16:35,935
Abrupt uterine decompression during membrane rupture may be an inciting factor.

133
00:16:35,935 --> 00:16:46,660
Prior Abruption Many of the predisposing factors in women with a history of an abruption are chronic conditions, and in these cases, placental abruption has a high recurrence rate.

134
00:16:46,660 --> 00:16:54,448
Women with abruption and fetal death have a recurrence rate of 12 percent, and half of these abruptions caused another fetal death .

135
00:16:54,448 --> 00:17:05,498
Furuhashi and colleagues (2002) reported a 22-percent recurrence rate, and half recurred at a gestational age 1 to 3 weeks earlier than the first abruption.

136
00:17:05,498 --> 00:17:14,135
In one longitudinal Dutch study, Ruiter and coworkers (2015) cited a recurrence risk of 5.8 percent.

137
00:17:14,135 --> 00:17:26,210
In a populationbased study of 767,000 pregnancies, the authors found a sevenfold higher risk for recurrence of a “mild” abruption and twelvefold risk for a “severe” abruption .

138
00:17:26,210 --> 00:17:32,110
For women who had two severe abruptions, the risk for a third was increased 50-fold.

139
00:17:32,110 --> 00:17:40,285
Management of a pregnancy subsequent to an abruption is difficult because another separation may suddenly occur, even remote from term.

140
00:17:40,285 --> 00:17:47,335
In many of these recurrences, evaluation of fetal well-being is almost always reassuring beforehand.

141
00:17:47,335 --> 00:17:51,848
Thus, antepartum fetal testing is usually not predictive.

142
00:17:51,848 --> 00:18:01,185
Because term abruptions tend to be recurrent, Ruiter and coworkers (2015) recommend labor induction at 37 weeks’ gestation.

143
00:18:01,185 --> 00:18:08,410
It seems reasonable to induce labor at 38 completed weeks if other complications do not develop beforehand.

144
00:18:08,410 --> 00:18:17,148
Other Associations Of these listed in Table 43-1, cigarette smoking is linked to an elevated risk for placental abruption .

145
00:18:17,148 --> 00:18:23,223
One metaanalysis of 1.6 million pregnancies in smokers found a twofold risk .

146
00:18:23,223 --> 00:18:30,398
This risk was five- to eightfold if smokers had chronic hypertension, severe preeclampsia, or both.

147
00:18:30,398 --> 00:18:37,448
Similar findings are reported by others (Hogberg, 2007; Kaminsky, 2007).

148
00:18:37,448 --> 00:18:46,573
Cocaine abuse is linked, and in one series of 50 women who abused cocaine during pregnancy, eight had a stillbirth caused by placental abruption .

149
00:18:46,573 --> 00:18:55,110
Of potential serum markers, MSAFP, inhibin, and pregnancy-associated plasma protein in abnormal levels carry increased risk .

150
00:18:55,110 --> 00:19:02,073
Indeed, preliminary data shows that MSAFP levels >280 µg/L in the third

151
00:19:02,236 --> 00:19:04,798
trimester may be predictive .

152
00:19:04,748 --> 00:19:18,661
Subclinical hypothyroidism or high levels of antithyroid antibodies have been associated with a two- to threefold higher risk for placental abruption (Abbassi-Ghanavati, 2010; Maraka, 2016).

153
00:19:18,661 --> 00:19:26,286
Lupus anticoagulant is associated with maternal floor infarction of the placenta but is less so with typical abruptions.

154
00:19:26,286 --> 00:19:44,861
Women affected by some of the thrombophilias have higher associated rates of thromboembolic events during pregnancy, however, no convincing evidence supports a link between thrombophilias and placental abruption (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019; 2020).

155
00:19:44,861 --> 00:19:47,261
# Diagnosis.

156
00:19:47,261 --> 00:19:51,873
The signs and symptoms of placental abruption can vary considerably.

157
00:19:51,873 --> 00:19:59,098
Classically, affected women have a sudden onset of abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and uterine tenderness.

158
00:19:59,098 --> 00:20:10,686
In an earlier study, 78 percent had vaginal bleeding, 66 percent had uterine tenderness or back pain, and 60 percent had a nonreassuring fetal status .

159
00:20:10,686 --> 00:20:15,761
Other findings included frequent contractions and persistent hypertonus.

160
00:20:15,761 --> 00:20:24,561
However, in a fifth, preterm labor was diagnosed, and placental abruption was not suspected until fetal distress or death followed.

161
00:20:24,561 --> 00:20:32,923
In some women, external bleeding can be profuse, yet placental separation may not be so extensive as to compromise the fetus.

162
00:20:32,923 --> 00:20:40,823
In others, external bleeding is absent, but the placenta is sufficiently sheared off to kill the fetus—a concealed abruption.

163
00:20:40,823 --> 00:20:45,911
In either case, bleeding can be massive and leads to hypovolemic shock.

164
00:20:45,911 --> 00:20:56,898
In an earlier report from Parkland Hospital, Pritchard and Brekken (1967) described 141 women with abruption so severe as to kill the fetus.

165
00:20:56,898 --> 00:21:02,173
Blood loss in these women often amounted to at least half of their pregnant blood volume.

166
00:21:02,173 --> 00:21:10,073
Consumptive Coagulopathy With placental abruption, some degree of intravascular coagulation is almost universal.

167
00:21:10,073 --> 00:21:18,736
Placental abruption is the most common cause of clinically profound consumptive coagulopathy in obstetrics and probably in all specialties .

168
00:21:18,736 --> 00:21:30,361
A primary consequence of intravascular coagulation is the activation of plasminogen to plasmin, which then lyses fibrin microemboli to maintain microcirculatory patency.

169
00:21:30,361 --> 00:21:41,573
With placental abruption severe enough to kill the fetus, pathological levels of fibrinogen– fibrin degradation products and D-dimers are almost always found in maternal serum .

170
00:21:41,573 --> 00:21:44,961
Their quantification is not clinically useful.

171
00:21:44,961 --> 00:21:54,323
In a third of women with an abruption severe enough to kill the fetus, the plasma fibrinogen level will be <150 mg/dL.

172
00:21:54,323 --> 00:22:05,261
These levels are dependent on the maternal preabruption fibrinogen level, and thus higher levels are “protective” (Cunningham, 2015; Wang, 2016).

173
00:22:05,261 --> 00:22:13,736
Clinically significant low levels may cause troublesome surgical bleeding, and measurement of levels assists fibrinogen-replacement efforts.

174
00:22:13,736 --> 00:22:18,948
Levels of several other coagulation factors also are variably decreased.

175
00:22:18,948 --> 00:22:28,523
These are not specifically measured in DIC, but factors are replaced empirically with fresh frozen plasma as a part of massive transfusion protocols .

176
00:22:28,523 --> 00:22:38,536
In addition, thrombocytopenia, sometimes profound, may accompany severe hypofibrinogenemia and can be common after repeated blood transfusions.

177
00:22:38,536 --> 00:22:42,923
This “dilutional coagulopathy” is additive to DIC .

178
00:22:42,923 --> 00:22:49,523
Consumptive coagulopathy is more likely with a concealed abruption because intrauterine pressure is higher.

179
00:22:49,523 --> 00:22:55,298
This forces more thromboplastin into the large veins draining the implantation site.

180
00:22:55,298 --> 00:23:01,386
With a partial abruption and a live fetus, severe coagulation defects are less common.

181
00:23:01,386 --> 00:23:09,273
Our experience has been that if serious coagulopathy develops, it is usually evident by the time abruption symptoms appear.

182
00:23:09,273 --> 00:23:15,161
In one unusual case, a woman cared for at Parkland Hospital presented with a nosebleed.

183
00:23:15,161 --> 00:23:19,573
She had no pain or vaginal bleeding, but her fetus was dead.

184
00:23:19,573 --> 00:23:25,986
Her blood did not clot, and the plasma fibrinogen level was 25 mg/dL.

185
00:23:25,986 --> 00:23:29,873
A total placental abruption was confirmed at delivery.

186
00:23:29,873 --> 00:23:40,298
Couvelaire Uterus At the time of cesarean delivery, it is not uncommon to find widespread extravasation of blood into the uterine musculature and beneath the serosa .

187
00:23:40,298 --> 00:23:42,986
This phenomenon is named after

188
00:23:43,156 --> 00:23:48,668
Couvelaire, who in the early 1900s termed it uteroplacental apoplexy.

189
00:23:48,618 --> 00:23:55,581
These myometrial hemorrhages may incite uterine atony, but are not a sole indication for hysterectomy.

190
00:23:55,581 --> 00:24:04,568
Effusions of blood may collect beneath the tubal serosa, between the leaves of the broad ligaments, in the ovaries, or in the peritoneal cavity.

191
00:24:04,568 --> 00:24:07,306
Figure 43-5.

192
00:24:07,306 --> 00:24:12,256
Couvelaire uterus from total placental abruption after cesarean delivery.

193
00:24:12,256 --> 00:24:16,931
Blood markedly infiltrates the myometrium to reach the serosa.

194
00:24:16,931 --> 00:24:21,093
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Steven Blaine Holloway.

195
00:24:21,093 --> 00:24:29,068
End-organ Injury Acute kidney injury (AKI) is a general term describing renal dysfunction from many causes .

196
00:24:29,068 --> 00:24:33,143
Delayed or incomplete treatment of hypovolemia is one.

197
00:24:33,143 --> 00:24:44,556
However, even with placental abruption complicated by severe DIC, prompt and vigorous treatment with blood and crystalloid solutions usually prevents significant renal dysfunction.

198
00:24:44,556 --> 00:24:54,656
The risk for renal injury with placental abruption rises when preeclampsia coexists (Alexander, 2015; Drakeley, 2002).

199
00:24:54,656 --> 00:25:02,418
Most cases of AKI are reversible, do not require dialysis, and generally have good long-term outcomes .

200
00:25:02,418 --> 00:25:08,543
However, irreversible acute cortical necrosis occasionally stems from placental abruption .

201
00:25:08,543 --> 00:25:16,006
Rarely, pituitary failure—Sheehan syndrome—follows severe intrapartum or early postpartum hemorrhage.

202
00:25:16,006 --> 00:25:29,406
Described in Chapter 61 (p. 1104), the exact pathogenesis is not well understood, especially because endocrine abnormalities are infrequent even in women who suffer catastrophic hemorrhage .

203
00:25:29,406 --> 00:25:34,418
Differential Diagnosis Severe placental abruption usually is obvious.

204
00:25:34,418 --> 00:25:42,018
However, less severe forms are not always recognized with certainty, and the diagnosis becomes one of exclusion.

205
00:25:42,018 --> 00:25:50,268
Unfortunately, no laboratory tests or other diagnostic methods accurately confirm lesser degrees of placental separation.

206
00:25:50,268 --> 00:25:57,218
Sonography has limited use because the placenta and fresh clots may have similar imaging characteristics.

207
00:25:57,218 --> 00:26:05,756
In a study of 149 women with a suspected placental abruption, the sensitivity for sonography was only 24 percent .

208
00:26:05,756 --> 00:26:11,931
Thus, negative findings with sonographic examination do not exclude placental abruption.

209
00:26:11,931 --> 00:26:22,131
Conversely, magnetic resonance (MR) imaging is highly sensitive for placental abruption and should be considered if the diagnostic information would change management .

210
00:26:22,131 --> 00:26:29,068
Last, elevated serum levels of D-dimers may be suggestive, but this has not been adequately tested.

211
00:26:29,068 --> 00:26:38,893
Thus, in the woman with vaginal bleeding and a live fetus, placenta previa and other bleeding causes are sought with clinical and sonographic evaluation.

212
00:26:38,893 --> 00:26:50,143
It has long been taught—perhaps with some justification—that painful uterine bleeding signifies placental abruption, whereas painless uterine bleeding indicates placenta previa.

213
00:26:50,143 --> 00:26:59,118
The differential diagnosis is usually not this straightforward, and labor accompanying previa may cause pain that suggests placental abruption.

214
00:26:59,118 --> 00:27:07,393
Conversely, pain from placental abruption may mimic normal labor, or it may be painless, especially with a posterior placenta.

215
00:27:07,393 --> 00:27:12,731
At times, the vaginal bleeding source remains obscure even after delivery.

216
00:27:12,731 --> 00:27:14,956
# Management.

217
00:27:14,956 --> 00:27:22,468
Treatment varies depending on maternofetal clinical condition, gestational age, and amount of associated hemorrhage.

218
00:27:22,468 --> 00:27:30,606
With a living viable-aged fetus and with vaginal delivery not imminent, emergency cesarean delivery is chosen by most.

219
00:27:30,606 --> 00:27:36,656
In some women, fetal compromise will be evident as shown in Figure 43-6.

220
00:27:36,656 --> 00:27:50,443
When evaluating fetal status, sonographic confirmation of fetal heart activity may be necessary because sometimes an electrode applied directly to a dead fetus will provide misleading information by recording the maternal heart rate .

221
00:27:50,443 --> 00:27:53,193
Figure 43-6.

222
00:27:53,193 --> 00:27:56,456
Placental abruption with fetal compromise.

223
00:27:56,456 --> 00:28:14,181
Lower panel: Uterine hypertonus with a baseline pressure of 20 to 25 mm Hg and frequent contractions peaking at approximately 75 mm Hg. Upper panel: The fetal heart rate demonstrates baseline bradycardia with repetitive late decelerations.

224
00:28:14,181 --> 00:28:22,706
If the fetus has died or if it is not considered sufficiently mature to live outside the uterus, vaginal birth is preferable for the mother.

225
00:28:22,706 --> 00:28:25,793
In either case, prompt and intensive

226
00:28:25,948 --> 00:28:30,173
resuscitation with blood products and crystalloids is begun.

227
00:28:30,123 --> 00:28:34,735
These measures are lifesaving for the mother and hopefully for her fetus.

228
00:28:34,735 --> 00:28:46,310
If the diagnosis of placental abruption is uncertain and the fetus is alive and without evidence of compromise, close observation may be warranted provided that immediate intervention is available.

229
00:28:46,310 --> 00:29:00,223
Colón and coworkers (2016) performed a randomized trial and found no benefits to magnesium sulfate tocolysis given to women with a preterm “nonsevere” abruption at 24 to 34 weeks’ gestation.

230
00:29:00,223 --> 00:29:10,435
Cesarean Delivery The compromised fetus is usually best served by cesarean delivery, and the speed of response is an important factor in perinatal outcomes.

231
00:29:10,435 --> 00:29:24,510
In one study of 33 singleton pregnancies with a clinically overt placental abruption and fetal bradycardia, 15 of the 22 neurologically intact survivors were delivered within a 20-minute decision-to-delivery interval .

232
00:29:24,510 --> 00:29:31,848
However, eight of 11 infants who died or developed cerebral palsy were delivered after a 20-minute intervals.

233
00:29:31,848 --> 00:29:40,923
Onishi and colleagues (2019) also found a significant negative correlation between these intervals and cord arterial pH.

234
00:29:40,923 --> 00:29:49,635
At Parkland Hospital, placental abruption was one of the most powerful antecedents to body-cooling treatment for neonatal encephalopathy .

235
00:29:49,635 --> 00:29:56,435
A major hazard to cesarean delivery is imposed by clinically significant consumptive coagulopathy.

236
00:29:56,435 --> 00:30:03,185
Preparations include two large-bore intravenous catheters and plans for blood and component replacement.

237
00:30:03,185 --> 00:30:14,010
Measures of hemoglobin, platelet, and fibrinogen levels as well as protime (PT) and partial thromboplastin time (PTT) guide product replacement.

238
00:30:14,010 --> 00:30:19,748
Vaginal Delivery If the fetus has died, vaginal delivery is usually preferred.

239
00:30:19,748 --> 00:30:29,298
As reviewed earlier, hemostasis at the placental implantation site depends primarily on myometrial contraction and not blood coagulability.

240
00:30:29,298 --> 00:30:37,748
Thus, after vaginal delivery, uterotonic agents and uterine massage are used to stimulate myometrial contractions.

241
00:30:37,748 --> 00:30:45,235
Uterine muscle fibers compress placental site vessels and prompt hemostasis even if coagulation is defective.

242
00:30:45,235 --> 00:30:51,060
In some instances, vaginal delivery may not be preferable, even with a dead fetus.

243
00:30:51,060 --> 00:30:57,223
One example is brisk hemorrhage that cannot be successfully managed by vigorous blood replacement.

244
00:30:57,223 --> 00:31:03,035
Others are the myriad obstetrical complications that prohibit vaginal delivery in general.

245
00:31:03,035 --> 00:31:13,423
In some women with extensive placental abruption, labor tends to be rapid because the uterus is usually persistently hypertonic (see Fig. 43-6).

246
00:31:13,423 --> 00:31:16,535
This can magnify fetal compromise.

247
00:31:16,535 --> 00:31:33,910
In some cases, baseline intraamnionic pressures reach 50 mm Hg or higher, and with contractions, pressures may attain levels exceeding 100 mm Hg. Overall, however, first- and second-stage labor does not appear to be shorter than usual .

248
00:31:33,910 --> 00:31:39,173
Early amniotomy has long been championed in the management of placental abruption.

249
00:31:39,173 --> 00:31:49,510
This ostensibly achieves better spiral artery compression to diminish implantation site bleeding and reduce thromboplastin infusion into the maternal vascular system.

250
00:31:49,510 --> 00:31:55,223
Although evidence supporting this theory is lacking, membrane rupture may hasten delivery.

251
00:31:55,223 --> 00:32:02,148
However, if the fetus is small, the intact sac may be more efficient in promoting cervical dilation.

252
00:32:02,148 --> 00:32:10,248
If rhythmic uterine contractions are not superimposed on baseline hypertonus, oxytocin is given in standard doses.

253
00:32:10,248 --> 00:32:18,285
No data indicate that oxytocin augments thromboplastin escape into the maternal circulation to worsen coagulopathy.

254
00:32:18,285 --> 00:32:28,498
In 1ight of hypertonus associated with placental abruption, misoprostol may be a less favored induction agent due to its association with uterine tachysystole.

255
00:32:28,498 --> 00:32:33,910
In the past, some had set arbitrary time limits to permit vaginal delivery.

256
00:32:33,910 --> 00:32:44,548
Instead, experiences illustrate that maternal outcome depends on the diligence with which adequate fluid and blood replacement therapy are pursued rather than on the interval to delivery.

257
00:32:44,548 --> 00:32:56,585
Observations from Parkland Hospital described by Pritchard and Brekken (1967) are similar to those from the University of Virginia reported by Brame and associates (1968).

258
00:32:56,585 --> 00:33:00,935
Specifically, women with severe abruption who were transfused

259
00:33:01,084 --> 00:33:08,296
during 18 hours or more before delivery had similar outcomes to those in whom delivery was accomplished sooner.

260
00:33:08,246 --> 00:33:14,084
Expectant Management If possible, delaying delivery may benefit an immature fetus.

261
00:33:14,084 --> 00:33:24,521
In one series, 43 women with placental abruption before 35 weeks’ gestation were expectantly managed, and 31 of them were given tocolytic therapy .

262
00:33:24,521 --> 00:33:29,796
The mean interval until delivery for all 43 approximated 12 days.

263
00:33:29,796 --> 00:33:35,509
Cesarean delivery was performed in 75 percent, and there were no stillbirths.

264
00:33:35,509 --> 00:33:42,946
As discussed earlier, women with a very early abruption may develop chronic abruption–oligohydramnios sequence.

265
00:33:42,946 --> 00:33:54,521
In one report, four women with an abruption at a mean gestational age of 20 weeks subsequently developed oligohydramnios and delivered at an average gestational age of 28 weeks .

266
00:33:54,521 --> 00:34:03,446
In another description of 256 women with an abruption at <28 weeks’ gestation, a mean of 1.6 weeks was gained .

267
00:34:03,446 --> 00:34:11,746
Of the group, 65 percent were delivered before 29 weeks, and half of all women underwent emergent cesarean delivery.

268
00:34:11,746 --> 00:34:18,771
Unfortunately, even continuous fetal heart rate monitoring does not guarantee universally good outcomes.

269
00:34:18,771 --> 00:34:24,471
A normal tracing may precede sudden further separation with instant fetal compromise.

270
00:34:24,471 --> 00:34:30,484
In some of these, if the separation is sufficient, the fetus will die before it can be delivered.

271
00:34:30,484 --> 00:34:36,934
Tocolysis is advocated by some for suspected abruption if the fetus does not display compromise.

272
00:34:36,934 --> 00:34:48,846
Some investigators have observed that tocolysis improved outcomes in a highly selected cohort of women with preterm pregnancies (Bond, 1989; Combs, 1992).

273
00:34:48,846 --> 00:35:02,921
In another study, Towers and coworkers (1999) administered magnesium sulfate, terbutaline, or both to 95 of 131 women with abruption diagnosed before 36 weeks’ gestation.

274
00:35:02,921 --> 00:35:08,746
The perinatal mortality rate was 5 percent in both groups with or without tocolysis.

275
00:35:08,746 --> 00:35:14,684
Similar results were reported from a randomized trial (Colón, 2016).

276
00:35:14,684 --> 00:35:24,071
A clinical consideration to the use of tocolytic agents, such as terbutaline, is that the drug-induced tachycardia may mask maternal compromise.

277
00:35:24,071 --> 00:35:30,096
We are of the opinion that suspected placental abruption contraindicates tocolytic agent use.

278
00:35:30,096 --> 00:35:32,096
Placenta Previa.

279
00:35:32,096 --> 00:35:34,546
# Classification.

280
00:35:34,546 --> 00:35:41,509
The Latin previa means going before, and in this sense, the placenta goes before the fetus into the birth canal.

281
00:35:41,509 --> 00:35:50,584
Placenta previa describes a placenta that is implanted somewhere in the lower uterine segment, either over or very near the internal cervical os.

282
00:35:50,584 --> 00:36:01,121
Because these anatomical relationships cannot always be precisely defined and because they frequently change across pregnancy, terminology can sometimes be confusing.

283
00:36:01,121 --> 00:36:20,171
Terminology for placenta previa has evolved, and the National Institutes of Health (NIH)-sponsored Fetal Imaging Workshop recommends the following classification: • Placenta previa: the internal os is covered partially or completely by placenta (Figs. 43-7 and 43-8).

284
00:36:20,171 --> 00:36:25,359
In the past, these were further classified as either total or partial previa.

285
00:36:25,359 --> 00:36:27,996
Figure 43-7.

286
00:36:27,996 --> 00:36:33,984
Placenta previa showing that copious hemorrhage could be anticipated with any cervical dilation.

287
00:36:33,984 --> 00:36:36,459
Figure 43-8.

288
00:36:36,459 --> 00:36:42,284
On speculum examination, placenta is visible protruding through the cervical os.

289
00:36:42,284 --> 00:36:57,421
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Maureen E. Flowers.) • Low-lying placenta: implantation in the lower uterine segment is such that the placental edge does not cover the internal os but lies within a 2-cm wide perimeter around the os.

290
00:36:57,421 --> 00:37:05,684
A previously used term, marginal previa, described a placenta that was at the edge of the internal os but did not overlie it.

291
00:37:05,684 --> 00:37:12,246
Clearly, the classification of some cases will depend on cervical dilation at the time of assessment.

292
00:37:12,246 --> 00:37:23,896
For example, a low-lying placenta at 2-cm dilation may become a partial placenta previa at 4-cm dilation because the cervix has opened to expose the placental edge.

293
00:37:23,896 --> 00:37:35,921
Conversely, a placenta previa that appears to be total before cervical dilation may become partial at 4-cm dilation because the cervical opening now extends beyond the edge of the placenta.

294
00:37:35,921 --> 00:37:44,521
Digital palpation in an attempt to ascertain these changing relations between the placental edge and internal os as the cervix dilates

295
00:37:44,692 --> 00:37:47,492
usually causes severe hemorrhage!

296
00:37:47,442 --> 00:37:57,929
With any degree of placenta previa, a certain amount of spontaneous placental separation is inevitable during lower uterine segment remodeling and cervical dilation.

297
00:37:57,929 --> 00:38:06,579
Although this frequently causes bleeding and thus technically constitutes a placental abruption, this term is usually not applied in these instances.

298
00:38:06,579 --> 00:38:14,554
Somewhat but not always related is vasa previa, in which fetal vessels course through membranes and present at the cervical os.

299
00:38:14,554 --> 00:38:24,442
Vasa previa has been reviewed by the Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine (2015) and is discussed in Chapter 6 (p. 115).

300
00:38:24,442 --> 00:38:27,192
# Placental Migration.

301
00:38:27,192 --> 00:38:37,142
Beginning with the use of sonography in obstetrics, the term placental migration was coined to describe the apparent movement of the placenta away from the internal os .

302
00:38:37,142 --> 00:38:44,604
Obviously, the placenta does not actually move, and the mechanism of apparent movement is not completely understood.

303
00:38:44,604 --> 00:38:51,479
To begin with, migration is clearly a misnomer, because decidual invasion anchors chorionic villi.

304
00:38:51,479 --> 00:38:55,704
Explanations of placental migration are likely additive.

305
00:38:55,704 --> 00:39:04,242
First, apparent movement of the low-lying placenta relative to the internal os is related to the imprecision of two-dimensional sonography.

306
00:39:04,242 --> 00:39:10,292
Second, as pregnancy progresses, growth of the lower and upper uterine segments differs.

307
00:39:10,292 --> 00:39:19,779
With greater blood flow in the upper uterus, placental growth toward this supply, termed trophotropism, is thus more likely directed toward the fundus.

308
00:39:19,779 --> 00:39:28,954
Many of those placentas that “migrate” most likely never were circumferentially implanted with true villous invasion that reached the internal cervical os.

309
00:39:28,954 --> 00:39:37,104
Importantly, a low-lying placenta or placenta previa is less likely to “migrate” if there is a prior cesarean delivery scar.

310
00:39:37,104 --> 00:39:41,254
The frequency of placental migration has been quantified.

311
00:39:41,254 --> 00:39:48,067
In one study of 4300 women at midpregnancy, 12 percent had a low-lying placenta .

312
00:39:48,067 --> 00:39:55,967
Of placentas not covering the internal os, previa did not persist, and none subsequently had placental hemorrhage.

313
00:39:55,967 --> 00:40:04,029
Conversely, approximately 40 percent of placentas that covered the os at midpregnancy continued to do so until delivery.

314
00:40:04,029 --> 00:40:17,004
Thus, placentas that lie close to but not over the internal os until the early third trimester are unlikely to persist as a previa by term (Heller, 2014; Parrott, 2015).

315
00:40:17,004 --> 00:40:26,092
The chance that placenta previa persists increases with a hysterotomy scar (Kohari, 2012; Oyelese, 2006).

316
00:40:26,092 --> 00:40:29,629
# Incidence and Associated Factors.

317
00:40:29,629 --> 00:40:34,404
The incidence of placenta previa has risen during the past 30 years.

318
00:40:34,404 --> 00:40:41,954
Reported incidences average about 0.4 percent or 1 case per 250 to 400 deliveries .

319
00:40:41,954 --> 00:40:51,829
The frequency at Parkland Hospital from 1988 through 2003 for nearly 250,000 births was 2.6 cases per 1000.

320
00:40:51,829 --> 00:40:58,292
For the 2004 to 2020 epoch, it rose to 3.8 cases per 1000.

321
00:40:58,292 --> 00:41:09,692
Similar frequencies have been reported from Austria, Finland, and Israel (Kollmann, 2016; Räisänen, 2014; Rosenberg, 2011).

322
00:41:09,692 --> 00:41:15,067
Several demographic factors may contribute to this higher rate of placenta previa.

323
00:41:15,067 --> 00:41:22,517
First, maternal age increases the frequency (Biro, 2012; Roberts, 2012).

324
00:41:22,517 --> 00:41:40,179
In the First- and Second-Trimester Evaluation of Risk (FASTER) trial, which included more than 36,000 women, the frequency of previa was 0.5 percent for women <35 years compared with 1.1 percent in those ≥35 years .

325
00:41:40,179 --> 00:41:55,279
At Parkland Hospital, the incidence ranged from a rate of approximately 0.65 cases per 1000 births for women ≤19 years to almost 10 cases per 1000 births for women older than 35.

326
00:41:55,279 --> 00:42:01,629
Multiparity also elevates the rate of placenta previa (Räisänen, 2014).

327
00:42:01,629 --> 00:42:06,967
Obviously, the effects of advancing maternal age and parity are confounding.

328
00:42:06,967 --> 00:42:18,817
Still, Babinszki and colleagues (1999) reported that the 2.2-percent incidence in women with parity of five or greater was significantly higher than that of women with lower parity.

329
00:42:18,817 --> 00:42:22,592
The interpregnancy interval does not affect this rate .

330
00:42:22,592 --> 00:42:28,329
Cigarette smoking increases the relative risk of placenta previa at least twofold .

331
00:42:28,329 --> 00:42:36,442
It has been postulated that carbon monoxide hypoxemia causes compensatory placental hypertrophy and greater surface area.

332
00:42:36,442 --> 00:42:40,692
Smoking may also be related to decidual vasculopathy.

333
00:42:40,692 --> 00:42:42,692
Several clinical

334
00:42:42,844 --> 00:42:46,781
characteristics also raise placenta previa risks.

335
00:42:46,731 --> 00:43:03,581
Foremost, women with one or more prior cesarean deliveries are at greater risk for subsequent placental disorders that include placenta previa, placental abruption, or placenta accreta spectrum (PAS) (Gibbins, 2018; Klar, 2014).

336
00:43:03,581 --> 00:43:10,756
The cumulative risks for placenta previa that accrue with the increasing number of cesarean deliveries are extraordinary.

337
00:43:10,756 --> 00:43:27,319
In one MFMU Network study of 30,132 women undergoing cesarean delivery, the incidence was 1.3 percent for those with only one prior cesarean delivery, but it was 3.4 percent if there were six or more prior cesareans .

338
00:43:27,319 --> 00:43:41,381
In a retrospective cohort of nearly 400,000 women who were delivered of two consecutive singletons, those with a cesarean delivery for the first pregnancy had a 1.6-fold greater rate for previa in the second pregnancy .

339
00:43:41,381 --> 00:43:48,594
These same investigators reported a 1.5-fold higher rate from six similar population-based studies.

340
00:43:48,594 --> 00:43:56,231
This risk is increased eightfold in women with parity greater than four and who have more than four prior cesarean deliveries .

341
00:43:56,231 --> 00:44:08,594
Importantly, women with a prior uterine incision and placenta previa have an increased likelihood that cesarean hysterectomy will be necessary because of associated PAS (p. 761).

342
00:44:08,594 --> 00:44:16,219
In one study, 6 percent of women with a primary cesarean delivery for placenta previa required a hysterectomy.

343
00:44:16,219 --> 00:44:22,694
This rate was 25 percent for women with a placenta previa undergoing repeat cesarean delivery .

344
00:44:22,694 --> 00:44:30,544
Multifetal gestation raises placenta previa risk (Ananth, 2003a; Luke, 2017a).

345
00:44:30,544 --> 00:44:39,119
Compared with monochorionic twins, dichorionic ones show higher rates, which perhaps stems from having two implantation sites .

346
00:44:39,119 --> 00:44:49,956
MSAFP levels, if abnormally elevated for otherwise unexplained reasons during prenatal screening, raise the risk for placenta previa and a host of other abnormalities.

347
00:44:49,956 --> 00:45:03,144
Moreover, women with a placenta previa and comorbid MSAFP level >2.0 multiples of the median (MoM) at 16 weeks’ gestation were at greater risk for late-pregnancy bleeding and preterm birth .

348
00:45:03,144 --> 00:45:09,719
Assisted reproductive technology (ART) used for conception elevates placenta previa rates.

349
00:45:09,719 --> 00:45:14,044
Some of this association may derive from overlapping effects.

350
00:45:14,044 --> 00:45:19,469
First, older women constitute a significant portion of patients electing ART .

351
00:45:19,469 --> 00:45:26,319
Second, even adjusting for multifetal gestation, ART is still associated with higher previa rates .

352
00:45:26,319 --> 00:45:36,694
Last, in one systematic review including nearly 256,000 births, uterine leiomyomas were associated with higher placenta previa rates .

353
00:45:36,694 --> 00:45:39,281
# Clinical Features.

354
00:45:39,281 --> 00:45:43,969
Painless bleeding is the most characteristic event with placenta previa.

355
00:45:43,969 --> 00:45:51,894
Bleeding usually does not develop until near the end of the second trimester or later, but it can begin even before midpregnancy.

356
00:45:51,894 --> 00:45:57,494
Undoubtedly, some late abortions are caused by an abnormally located placenta.

357
00:45:57,494 --> 00:46:05,406
Initial bleeding from a previa usually begins without warning, and this sentinel bleed is rarely so profuse as to prove fatal.

358
00:46:05,406 --> 00:46:08,506
Usually it ceases, only to recur.

359
00:46:08,506 --> 00:46:19,019
However, in perhaps 10 percent of women, particularly those with a placenta implanted near but not over the cervical os, bleeding is delayed until labor onset.

360
00:46:19,019 --> 00:46:24,006
Bleeding at this time varies in degree, and it may mimic placental abruption.

361
00:46:24,006 --> 00:46:31,494
In cases in which the placenta is located over the internal os, a specific sequence of events leads to bleeding.

362
00:46:31,494 --> 00:46:36,406
First, the uterine body remodels to form the lower uterine segment.

363
00:46:36,406 --> 00:46:42,931
With this, the internal os dilates, and some of the implanted placenta inevitably separates.

364
00:46:42,931 --> 00:46:52,731
Bleeding that ensues is augmented by the inherent inability of myometrial fibers in the lower uterine segment to contract and thereby constrict torn vessels.

365
00:46:52,731 --> 00:47:00,231
Similarly, bleeding from this lower-segment implantation site also frequently continues after placental delivery.

366
00:47:00,231 --> 00:47:05,369
Last, lacerations in the friable cervix and lower segment will bleed.

367
00:47:05,369 --> 00:47:11,294
These may be especially problematic following manual removal of a somewhat adhered placenta.

368
00:47:11,294 --> 00:47:14,719
Cervical length may alter the clinical course.

369
00:47:14,719 --> 00:47:26,644
Stafford (2010) but not Trudell (2013), both with their associates, found that a placenta previa and a third-trimester cervical length <30 mm

370
00:47:26,812 --> 00:47:32,099
increased the risks for hemorrhage, uterine activity, and preterm birth.

371
00:47:32,049 --> 00:47:48,949
Similarly, Friszer (2013) but not Trudell (2013), both with their coworkers, showed that women admitted for bleeding had a greater chance of delivery in the subsequent 7 days when the cervical length measured <25 mm sonographically.

372
00:47:48,949 --> 00:47:55,324
Placenta accreta spectrum is a frequent and serious complication associated with placenta previa.

373
00:47:55,324 --> 00:48:02,687
This abnormal placental attachment derives in part from poorly developed decidua that lines the lower uterine segment.

374
00:48:02,687 --> 00:48:12,087
Biswas and coworkers (1999) performed placental bed biopsies in 50 women with a placenta previa and in 50 control women.

375
00:48:12,087 --> 00:48:22,687
Infiltration of spiral arterioles by trophoblastic giant cells, rather than by the expected endovascular trophoblast cells, was found in half of specimens.

376
00:48:22,687 --> 00:48:29,412
In contrast, only 20 percent of biopsies from normally implanted placentas had these changes.

377
00:48:29,412 --> 00:48:37,837
In another study of 514 cases of placenta previa, abnormal placental attachment was identified in 7 percent .

378
00:48:37,837 --> 00:48:45,724
As discussed, placenta previa overlying a prior cesarean incision conveys a particularly high risk for PAS.

379
00:48:45,724 --> 00:48:53,224
Coagulopathy rarely complicate placenta previa, even when implantation site separation is extensive .

380
00:48:53,224 --> 00:49:05,849
Placental thromboplastin, which incites the intravascular coagulation seen with placental abruption, is presumed to readily escape through the cervical canal rather than be forced into the maternal circulation.

381
00:49:05,849 --> 00:49:11,112
The paucity of large myometrial veins in this area also may be protective.

382
00:49:11,112 --> 00:49:13,512
# Diagnosis.

383
00:49:13,512 --> 00:49:20,499
For uterine bleeding after midpregnancy, placenta previa and placental abruption are always considered.

384
00:49:20,499 --> 00:49:30,249
In one Canadian study, placenta previa accounted for 21 percent of women admitted from 22 to 28 weeks’ gestation with vaginal bleeding .

385
00:49:30,249 --> 00:49:36,449
Previa should not be excluded until sonographic evaluation has clearly proved its absence.

386
00:49:36,449 --> 00:49:46,799
If sonography is not readily available, diagnosis by digital cervical examination is done in an operating room and with preparations in place for operative delivery.

387
00:49:46,799 --> 00:49:50,499
We call these preparations a double set-up technique.

388
00:49:50,499 --> 00:49:54,812
Even the gentlest examination can cause torrential hemorrhage.

389
00:49:54,812 --> 00:50:01,424
Using standard sonographic techniques, quick and accurate placental localization can be accomplished .

390
00:50:01,424 --> 00:50:05,237
This is usually done with transabdominal sonography.

391
00:50:05,237 --> 00:50:19,412
If the placenta clearly overlies the cervix or if it lies away from the lower uterine segment, the examination has excellent sensitivity and negative predictive value (Olive, 2006; Quant, 2014).

392
00:50:19,412 --> 00:50:23,812
Obese women may hinder clear viewing of the lower uterine segment.

393
00:50:23,812 --> 00:50:32,837
A full bladder may artificially elongate the cervix and compress the lower uterine segment to give the impression that the placenta overlies the cervix.

394
00:50:32,837 --> 00:50:42,249
If placental location remains unclear, transvaginal sonography is the most accurate sonographic method and is safe even with vaginal bleeding .

395
00:50:42,249 --> 00:50:52,499
In a comprehensive study, the internal os was visualized in all cases with transvaginal sonography but in only 30 percent with transabdominal sonography .

396
00:50:52,499 --> 00:51:01,999
As discussed, if the placental edge lies <2 cm from the internal os, but not covering it, the placenta is considered low lying .

397
00:51:01,999 --> 00:51:09,799
In the absence of any other indication, sonography need not be frequently repeated simply to document placental position.

398
00:51:09,799 --> 00:51:23,699
At Parkland Hospital, women with placenta previa identified at 18 to 22 weeks’ gestation and a prior cesarean delivery are reevaluated sonographically at 28 weeks and again at 32 weeks if it persists.

399
00:51:23,699 --> 00:51:30,449
Those with a placenta previa but without prior cesarean delivery undergo reimaging at 32 weeks.

400
00:51:30,449 --> 00:51:41,512
Restriction of activity is not necessary unless a previa persists beyond 28 weeks’ gestation or if clinical findings such as bleeding or contractions develop before this time.

401
00:51:41,512 --> 00:51:52,337
At 32 weeks’ gestation, if the placental edge is still <2 cm from the os, transvaginal sonography is repeated at 36 weeks’ gestation.

402
00:51:52,337 --> 00:51:54,974
Figure 43-9.

403
00:51:54,974 --> 00:52:04,774
In this transvaginal image at 21 weeks’ gestation, the posterior placenta (P) completely covers the internal cervical os (arrowhead).

404
00:52:04,774 --> 00:52:08,162
The dotted line represents the cervical canal.

405
00:52:08,162 --> 00:52:10,399
Using MR imaging,

406
00:52:10,564 --> 00:52:16,276
several investigators report excellent visualization of placental abnormalities.

407
00:52:16,226 --> 00:52:26,639
However, it is unlikely that this modality will replace sonography for routine evaluation anytime soon given availability and cost differences compared with sonography.

408
00:52:26,639 --> 00:52:33,039
However, MR imaging is useful for evaluation of PAS (p. 761).

409
00:52:33,039 --> 00:52:35,264
# Management.

410
00:52:35,264 --> 00:52:44,564
Care with placenta previa is individualized, and three prominent factors include fetal maturity, associated labor, and bleeding severity.

411
00:52:44,564 --> 00:52:53,614
In one study of 214 women with a placenta previa, 43 percent had an emergency delivery, and half of these were preterm .

412
00:52:53,614 --> 00:53:01,639
Instead, if active bleeding subsides and the fetus is immature, close observation in an obstetrical unit is indicated.

413
00:53:01,639 --> 00:53:06,926
Data are sparse regarding tocolytic administration for uterine contractions.

414
00:53:06,926 --> 00:53:17,764
Although robust randomized trials are lacking, Bose and colleagues (2011) recommend that if tocolytics are given, they be limited to 48 hours of administration.

415
00:53:17,764 --> 00:53:28,101
As noted earlier, the physiological cardiovascular responses to tocolytic agents that include hypotension and tachycardia can mask maternal compromise.

416
00:53:28,101 --> 00:53:32,014
We categorically recommend against their use in this setting.

417
00:53:32,014 --> 00:53:41,964
After bleeding has ceased for approximately 2 days and the fetus is judged to be healthy, a woman can usually be discharged home with instructions for “pelvic rest.

418
00:53:41,964 --> 00:53:50,251
The Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine (2018) does not recommend routine cervical length screening in these women.

419
00:53:50,251 --> 00:53:59,314
Importantly, the woman and her family must appreciate the possibility of recurrent bleeding and be prepared for immediate transport back to the hospital.

420
00:53:59,314 --> 00:54:03,926
In other cases, prolonged hospitalization may be necessary.

421
00:54:03,926 --> 00:54:14,801
The frequency of emergency delivery in women with placenta previa ranges from 25 to 40 percent (Erfani, 2019; Gibbons, 2018).

422
00:54:14,801 --> 00:54:23,389
In properly selected patients, however, long-term inpatient care does not appear to add benefits compared with outpatient management .

423
00:54:23,389 --> 00:54:35,126
In one randomized study of 53 women who had a bleeding previa at 24 to 36 weeks’ gestation, maternal or fetal morbidity rates did not differ between management methods .

424
00:54:35,126 --> 00:54:43,126
Of all study women, 60 percent had recurrent bleeding, and half eventually required expeditious cesarean delivery.

425
00:54:43,126 --> 00:54:49,389
For women who are near term and who are not bleeding, plans are made for scheduled cesarean delivery.

426
00:54:49,389 --> 00:54:56,976
A planned delivery in a controlled setting is optimal, and timing balances fetal immaturity against antepartum hemorrhage.

427
00:54:56,976 --> 00:55:12,776
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine (2021) recommend delivery for otherwise uncomplicated placenta previa between 360/7 and 376/7 weeks.

428
00:55:12,776 --> 00:55:19,364
At Parkland Hospital, we usually perform elective cesarean delivery at 380/7 weeks.

429
00:55:19,364 --> 00:55:24,551
Recommendations for delivery of women with PAS are outlined in that section.

430
00:55:24,551 --> 00:55:26,664
# Delivery.

431
00:55:26,664 --> 00:55:31,439
Practically all women with placenta previa undergo cesarean delivery.

432
00:55:31,439 --> 00:55:40,851
Some recommend a vertical laparotomy incision to provide rapid entry if bleeding is torrential or if generous operating space is required for hysterectomy.

433
00:55:40,851 --> 00:55:50,114
As discussed, cesarean delivery is emergently performed in more than half because of hemorrhage, for which about a fourth require blood transfusion .

434
00:55:50,114 --> 00:55:59,939
Although a low transverse hysterotomy is usually possible, this may cause fetal bleeding if the placenta is implanted anteriorly and the placenta is incised.

435
00:55:59,939 --> 00:56:04,189
In such cases, fetal delivery should be expeditious .

436
00:56:04,189 --> 00:56:09,214
Thus, a vertical uterine incision may be preferable in some instances.

437
00:56:09,214 --> 00:56:16,976
In either case, even when the incision extends through the placenta, maternal or fetal outcomes are rarely compromised.

438
00:56:16,976 --> 00:56:27,589
Following placental removal, the implantation site may bleed uncontrollably due to poorly contracted smooth muscle, which is characteristic of the lower uterine segment.

439
00:56:27,589 --> 00:56:38,301
If hemostasis at the placental implantation site cannot be obtained by uterotonic agent administration and pressure, it can be oversewn with no. 0 chromic sutures.

440
00:56:38,301 --> 00:56:49,901
Cho and associates (1991) described placing interrupted sutures at 1-cm intervals to form a circle around the bleeding portion of the lower segment to control hemorrhage.

441
00:56:49,901 --> 00:56:51,414
Others

442
00:56:51,580 --> 00:57:03,105
have reported success with compression sutures that traverse and compress the anterior and posterior uterine wall (Mohamed, 2019; Sallam, 2019).

443
00:57:03,055 --> 00:57:14,342
Of other methods, Bakri or Foley balloon tamponade used alone or coupled with compression sutures has been described (Albayrak, 2011; Diemert, 2012).

444
00:57:14,342 --> 00:57:24,117
Other surgical options are bilateral uterine or internal iliac artery ligation, illustrated in Chapter 44 (p. 779).

445
00:57:24,117 --> 00:57:28,267
Pelvic artery embolization also has gained acceptance.

446
00:57:28,267 --> 00:57:50,242
Yu and colleagues (2020) performed a randomized trial of perioperative prophylactic internal iliac artery balloon occlusion during cesarean delivery for placenta previa and found that deployment did not reduce postpartum hemorrhage or alter maternal or neonatal morbidity. If these more conservative methods fail and bleeding is brisk, hysterectomy is necessary.

447
00:57:50,242 --> 00:58:03,892
Placenta previa—especially with PAS—currently is a common indication for peripartum hysterectomy at Parkland Hospital and other institutions (Jakobsson, 2015; Wong, 2011).

448
00:58:03,892 --> 00:58:10,767
In cases without PAS, the reported incidence of hysterectomy with placenta previa is 2 percent .

449
00:58:10,767 --> 00:58:20,155
Thus, it is not possible to accurately estimate the effect on the hysterectomy rate from placenta previa alone without considering the associated PAS.

450
00:58:20,155 --> 00:58:32,667
Again, for women whose placenta previa is implanted anteriorly at the site of a prior uterine incision, the likelihood of an associated morbidly adherent placenta and need for hysterectomy is increased.

451
00:58:32,667 --> 00:58:41,692
In a study of 318 peripartum hysterectomies performed in the United Kingdom, 40 percent were done for abnormal placentation .

452
00:58:41,692 --> 00:58:49,617
At Parkland Hospital, 44 percent of cesarean hysterectomies were done for bleeding placenta previa or for PAS .

453
00:58:49,617 --> 00:58:55,792
The technique for peripartum hysterectomy is described in Chapter 30 (p. 560).

454
00:58:55,792 --> 00:58:59,205
# Maternal and Perinatal Outcomes.

455
00:58:59,205 --> 00:59:06,667
Placenta previa and coexistent PAS both contribute substantively to maternal morbidity and mortality rates.

456
00:59:06,667 --> 00:59:12,967
The maternal mortality ratio is increased approximately threefold for women with a placenta previa .

457
00:59:12,967 --> 00:59:29,417
In a report of 5367 maternal deaths in the United States from 2006 to 2013, placenta previa alone accounted for nearly 3 percent of deaths from hemorrhage (Creanga, 2014, 2017).

458
00:59:29,417 --> 00:59:36,580
The report from the Consortium on Safe Labor emphasizes the ongoing perinatal morbidity with placenta previa .

459
00:59:36,580 --> 00:59:48,067
In these cases, preterm delivery continues to be a major cause of perinatal death (Balachandar, 2020; Nørgaard, 2012; Salihu, 2003).

460
00:59:48,067 --> 00:59:56,980
Ananth and colleagues (2003b) reported a comparably elevated risk of neonatal death even for fetuses who delivered at term.

461
00:59:56,980 --> 01:00:09,067
This is at least partially related to the fetal anomaly rate, which is two- to threefold higher in pregnancies with placenta previa (Crane, 1999; Kancherla, 2015).

462
01:00:09,067 --> 01:00:16,555
The association of fetal-growth restriction with placenta previa is likely minimal after controlling for gestational age.

463
01:00:16,555 --> 01:00:27,267
In a population-based cohort of more than 500,000 singleton births, most low-birthweight newborns associated with placenta previa resulted from preterm birth .

464
01:00:27,267 --> 01:00:36,367
From their large metaanalysis, Balayla and coworkers (2019) found a slightly increased frequency of fetal-growth restriction.

465
01:00:36,367 --> 01:00:44,355
At least two other studies reported an elevated risk (Räisänen, 2014; Weiner, 2016).

466
01:00:44,355 --> 01:00:49,792
Apparently, fetal growth effects are similar with placenta previa and PAS .

467
01:00:49,792 --> 01:00:52,292
Placenta Accreta Spectrum.

468
01:00:52,292 --> 01:01:02,492
The term placenta accreta spectrum (PAS) describes aberrant placentation characterized by abnormally implanted, invasive, or adhered placenta.

469
01:01:02,492 --> 01:01:09,030
Derivation of accreta comes from the Latin ac- + crescere—to adhere or become attached to .

470
01:01:09,030 --> 01:01:19,142
It is also referred to as the morbidly adherent placenta, and in Europe it is referred to as a pernicious placenta previa with accreta and abnormally adherent placenta.

471
01:01:19,142 --> 01:01:22,080
# Etiopathogenesis.

472
01:01:22,080 --> 01:01:29,030
The major clinical problem is placental failure to separate normally from the myometrium after fetal delivery.

473
01:01:29,030 --> 01:01:41,542
This abnormal adherence stems in part from partial or total absence of the decidua basalis and imperfect development of the fibrinoid or Nitabuch layer, described in Chapter 5 (p. 85).

474
01:01:41,542 --> 01:01:44,955
If the decidual spongy layer is lacking either

475
01:01:45,124 --> 01:01:52,674
partially or totally, the physiological line of cleavage is absent, and some or all cotyledons are densely anchored.

476
01:01:52,624 --> 01:01:59,224
Microscopically, placental villi attach to smooth muscle fibers rather than to decidual cells.

477
01:01:59,224 --> 01:02:04,349
This decidual deficiency then prevents normal separation after delivery.

478
01:02:04,349 --> 01:02:15,261
The surface area involved at the implantation site and the depth of trophoblast ingrowth vary between women, but all affected placentas can potentially cause significant hemorrhage.

479
01:02:15,261 --> 01:02:21,624
Data now suggest that PAS is not solely caused by the decidua’s anatomical deficiency.

480
01:02:21,624 --> 01:02:32,099
Indeed, the cytotrophoblasts may control decidual invasion through factors such as angiogenesis (Duzyj, 2018; Goh, 2016).

481
01:02:32,099 --> 01:02:38,049
Moreover, some tissue specimens show immunologically mediated “hyperinvasiveness” .

482
01:02:38,049 --> 01:02:47,124
Some genes that code for remodeling and for adherence may be highly expressed (Matsukawa, 2019; Shainker, 2020).

483
01:02:47,124 --> 01:02:58,674
Myometrial fibers attached to the basal plate in an antecedent pregnancy are predictive markers for a subsequent PAS (Linn, 2015; Miller, 2016).

484
01:02:58,674 --> 01:03:04,549
This implies an antecedent “constitutional endometrial defect” in most cases.

485
01:03:04,549 --> 01:03:17,049
The greater risk conveyed by previous surgical uterine trauma may be partially explained by an enhanced vulnerability to trophoblast invasion (Einerson, 2020; Jauniaux, 2018).

486
01:03:17,049 --> 01:03:28,386
This association with prior trauma is reinforced by the close relationship between cesarean scar pregnancy (CSP) and later development of PAS in the same pregnancy.

487
01:03:28,386 --> 01:03:39,336
Indeed, accruing evidence suggests that CSP and PAS lie on a spectrum and that CSP is a precursor (Happe, 2020; Timor-Tritsch, 2019).

488
01:03:39,336 --> 01:03:56,949
Described in Chapter 12 (p. 229), early rupture and hemorrhage with CSP are not uncommon, and women often elect pregnancy-terminating interventions to avoid these (Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine, 2020; Timor-Tritsch, 2019).

489
01:03:56,949 --> 01:03:59,399
# Classification.

490
01:03:59,399 --> 01:04:07,386
Variants of PAS are classified by the depth of trophoblastic growth (Figs. 4310 and 43-11).

491
01:04:07,386 --> 01:04:12,336
Placenta accreta indicates that villi are attached to the myometrium.

492
01:04:12,336 --> 01:04:22,711
With placenta increta, villi actually invade the myometrium, and placenta percreta defines villi that penetrate through the myometrium and to or through the serosa .

493
01:04:22,711 --> 01:04:30,711
In clinical practice, these three variants are encountered in an approximate ratio of 80:15:5, respectively .

494
01:04:30,711 --> 01:04:38,399
At a referral center, however, the distribution was 22, 24, and 54 percent respectively .

495
01:04:38,399 --> 01:04:45,136
In all three varieties, abnormal adherence may involve all lobules—total placenta accreta.

496
01:04:45,136 --> 01:04:51,874
If all or part of a single lobule is abnormally attached, it is described as focal placenta accreta.

497
01:04:51,874 --> 01:04:59,886
Histological diagnosis cannot be made from the placenta alone, and myometrial samples are necessary for confirmation .

498
01:04:59,886 --> 01:05:09,161
Terminology for consistent classification has been lacking because of varying clinical criteria and lack of detailed pathological examinations.

499
01:05:09,161 --> 01:05:19,299
To alleviate this, the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) has recently proposed a clinical classification system for PAS .

500
01:05:19,299 --> 01:05:21,874
Figure 43-10.

501
01:05:21,874 --> 01:05:24,374
Placenta accreta spectrum.

502
01:05:24,374 --> 01:05:26,649
A. Placenta accreta.

503
01:05:26,649 --> 01:05:29,099
B. Placenta increta.

504
01:05:29,099 --> 01:05:31,636
C. Placenta percreta.

505
01:05:31,636 --> 01:05:34,336
Figure 43-11.

506
01:05:34,336 --> 01:05:40,061
Varying degrees of myometrial invasion with placenta accreta spectrum disorders.

507
01:05:40,061 --> 01:05:44,974
Incisions begin on the serosal surface and extend through to the placenta.

508
01:05:44,974 --> 01:05:51,461
A. In this case, the myometrium (M) shows minimal invasion by the placenta (P).

509
01:05:51,461 --> 01:05:54,161
S = uterine serosa.

510
01:05:54,161 --> 01:05:58,386
B. A greater degree of myometrial invasion is seen here.

511
01:05:58,386 --> 01:06:05,774
C. In this example, the placenta (brackets) extends to the serosal edge, held by the surgeon’s hand.

512
01:06:05,774 --> 01:06:08,899
No myometrium remains at this site.

513
01:06:08,899 --> 01:06:17,361
Reproduced with permission from Dr. C. Edward Wells in Cunningham FG: Placenta previa and morbidly adherent placenta.

514
01:06:17,361 --> 01:06:26,674
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd edition.

515
01:06:26,674 --> 01:06:31,111
New York, McGraw Hill Education, 2017.

516
01:06:31,111 --> 01:06:34,149
# Incidence and Risk Factors.

517
01:06:34,149 --> 01:06:39,174
Decades ago, the frequency of PAS was 1 in 20,000 births .

518
01:06:39,174 --> 01:06:48,324
As late as 1971, Hellman and Pritchard in the 14th edition of Williams Obstetrics described PAS as the subject of case reports.

519
01:06:48,324 --> 01:06:52,636
Since then, the incidence has grown remarkably in direct

520
01:06:52,804 --> 01:06:56,616
relationship to the rising cesarean delivery rate.

521
01:06:56,566 --> 01:07:11,216
For example, the incidence was 1 case in 2500 births in the 1980s, but it was 1 case per 731 births in a report from the MFMU Network comprising 115,502 women .

522
01:07:11,216 --> 01:07:18,216
From the Nationwide Inpatient Sample, the PAS rate was an astounding 1 case in 270 births .

523
01:07:18,216 --> 01:07:24,204
This rising frequency has made PAS one of the most formidable problems in obstetrics.

524
01:07:24,204 --> 01:07:39,541
Of 5367 pregnancy-related maternal deaths in the United States from 2006 to 2013, 13 percent were due to hemorrhage caused by PAS (Creanga, 2015, 2017).

525
01:07:39,541 --> 01:07:50,916
In addition, PAS is a leading cause of hemorrhage and emergency peripartum hysterectomy (Awan, 2011; Eller, 2011; Rossi, 2010).

526
01:07:50,916 --> 01:08:01,166
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine (2018) have outlined optimal management.

527
01:08:01,166 --> 01:08:07,341
The International Society for Abnormally Invasive Placenta also has published guidelines .

528
01:08:07,341 --> 01:08:12,291
In subsequent pregnancies following PAS, recurrence risks are high.

529
01:08:12,291 --> 01:08:22,604
Women in whom hysterectomy is avoided have an estimated 20-percent incidence of recurrence (Cunningham, 2016; Roeca, 2017).

530
01:08:22,604 --> 01:08:37,179
Some evidence shows that these women also have greater risks for placenta previa, uterine rupture, manual placental removal, preterm delivery, and hysterectomy (Baldwin, 2020; Eshkoli, 2013).

531
01:08:37,179 --> 01:08:43,466
PAS risk factors are similar to those for placenta previa (p. 756).

532
01:08:43,466 --> 01:08:57,491
Shown in Figure 43-12, the two most important elements are an associated placenta previa, a prior cesarean delivery, or more likely both (Klar, 2014; Silver, 2006).

533
01:08:57,491 --> 01:09:04,266
A classical hysterotomy incision has a higher risk for subsequent PAS than a lower uterine segment one .

534
01:09:04,266 --> 01:09:16,654
Of women with a prior cesarean delivery, almost half had myometrial fibers seen microscopically adhered to the placenta (Hardardottir, 1996; Miller, 2016).

535
01:09:16,654 --> 01:09:27,779
Dysfunctional decidua may also follow other myometrial trauma such as curettage or endometrial ablation (Baldwin, 2018; Gill, 2015).

536
01:09:27,779 --> 01:09:31,279
ART is an independent risk factor for PAS .

537
01:09:31,279 --> 01:09:33,904
Figure 43-12.

538
01:09:33,904 --> 01:09:42,691
Frequency of placenta accreta spectrum in women with one to four prior cesarean deliveries (CDs) and now with a placenta previa.

539
01:09:42,691 --> 01:09:52,141
Other risk markers are prenatal MSAFP and human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) used to screen for neural-tube defects and aneuploidies.

540
01:09:52,141 --> 01:10:10,129
In one study of more than 9300 women screened at 14 to 22 weeks’ gestation, the risk for PAS was eightfold higher with MSAFP levels >2.5 MoM, and it was increased fourfold with maternal serum free β-hCG levels >2.5 MoM .

541
01:10:10,129 --> 01:10:12,529
# Diagnosis.

542
01:10:12,529 --> 01:10:19,241
With first- and second-trimester PAS, hemorrhage usually stems from a coexisting placenta previa.

543
01:10:19,241 --> 01:10:29,166
Such bleeding will typically prompt evaluation, and ideally sonography is used (Chantraine, 2013; Jauniaux, 2018).

544
01:10:29,166 --> 01:10:39,316
With lesser degrees of bleeding, first-trimester measurement of the smallest myometrial thickness can help predict the later risk for peripartum hysterectomy with PAS .

545
01:10:39,316 --> 01:10:48,766
In a screening study of more than 22,000 singleton pregnancies at 11 to 13 weeks’ gestation, 6 percent were at high risk for PAS .

546
01:10:48,766 --> 01:10:54,054
However imaging was less than perfect to identify all of these placentas early.

547
01:10:54,054 --> 01:11:01,629
Subsequently, only 14 had a suspected clinical diagnosis, and 13 of these were confirmed at delivery.

548
01:11:01,629 --> 01:11:24,316
Five characteristic sonographic findings suggest PAS: (1) placental lacunae; (2) thinning of the retroplacental myometrium; (3) disruption of the bladder-uterine serosal interface; (4) bridging vessels from the placenta to the bladder-serosal interface; and (5) placental bulge that pushes outward and distorts the contour of the uterus .

549
01:11:24,316 --> 01:11:36,279
In a metaanalysis, the sensitivities of these criteria to identify placenta accreta, increta, and percreta were 91, 93, and 89 percent, respectively.

550
01:11:36,279 --> 01:11:42,929
Corresponding specificities were 97, 98, and 99 percent, respectively .

551
01:11:42,929 --> 01:11:45,804
Figure 43-13.

552
01:11:45,804 --> 01:11:52,866
Transabdominal sonogram of placental percreta shows multiple and massive placental “lakes” or “lacunae” (L).

553
01:11:52,866 --> 01:11:59,654
These figures may be overestimated because of clinical bias and considerable interobserver variability .

554
01:11:59,654 --> 01:12:08,841
Some investigators report fewer spectacular results with sonography (Jauniaux, 2016; Primo, 2014).

555
01:12:08,841 --> 01:12:10,929
Bowman and colleagues

556
01:12:11,092 --> 01:12:27,479
2014) described the sensitivity of sonography to be 54 percent; specificity, 88 percent; positive predictive value, 82 percent; negative predictive value, 65 percent; and accuracy, 65 percent.

557
01:12:27,479 --> 01:12:30,854
Location affects sonographic accuracy.

558
01:12:30,854 --> 01:12:41,954
Posterior placental location is associated with delayed diagnosis and increased surgical complications (Morgan, 2019; Tinari, 2021).

559
01:12:41,954 --> 01:12:50,342
In one study, the detection rate was 90 percent for anterior placenta accreta compared with 50 percent for posterior ones .

560
01:12:50,342 --> 01:12:59,579
Nageotte (2014) concluded that identification of PAS with sonography should be interpreted with clinical and operative findings.

561
01:12:59,579 --> 01:13:06,342
Better results have been found by some using three-dimensional (3-D) sonography and power Doppler .

562
01:13:06,342 --> 01:13:12,804
We too have found that the addition of Doppler color flow mapping aids prediction of myometrial invasion .

563
01:13:12,804 --> 01:13:27,779
Invasion is suspected if the distance between the uterine serosa–bladder wall interface and the retroplacental vessels measures <1 mm and if large intraplacental lacunae are seen (Rac, 2015a; Twickler, 2000).

564
01:13:27,779 --> 01:13:37,879
In another study, hypervascularity of the uterine serosa– bladder wall interface had the highest positive and negative predictive values for placenta percreta .

565
01:13:37,879 --> 01:13:44,342
Intracervical lakes also have been reported with placenta percreta (di Pasquo, 2020).

566
01:13:44,342 --> 01:13:47,192
Figure 43-14.

567
01:13:47,192 --> 01:13:52,129
Transvaginal sonogram of placental invasion with a placenta percreta.

568
01:13:52,129 --> 01:14:01,704
Retroplacental vessels (color Doppler) invade the myometrium, and abnormal intraplacental venous lakes (L) are commonly seen in this setting.

569
01:14:01,704 --> 01:14:08,492
Yule and coworkers (2020) reported that the placenta accreta index was useful for diagnosis.

570
01:14:08,492 --> 01:14:18,592
This index incorporates the number of prior cesarean deliveries, placental location, lacunae, bridging vessels, and smallest myometrial distance.

571
01:14:18,592 --> 01:14:25,554
These investigators are also exploring first-trimester color mapping to develop a quantification algorithm .

572
01:14:25,554 --> 01:14:29,892
To further delineate anatomy, MR imaging can be added.

573
01:14:29,892 --> 01:14:38,892
We and others use it to identify invasion of adjacent structures (Chalubinski, 2013; Reddy, 2014).

574
01:14:38,892 --> 01:14:45,554
Interobserver variability was reported to be excellent to diagnose PAS and assess invasion depth.

575
01:14:45,554 --> 01:14:49,567
There was less agreement in assessing topography of invasion .

576
01:14:49,567 --> 01:14:57,917
Using MR imaging, Mori and colleagues (2020) described a high prevalence of pelvic parasitic arteries with PAS.

577
01:14:57,917 --> 01:15:03,104
However, not all investigators have found MR imaging to be beneficial .

578
01:15:03,104 --> 01:15:09,567
Although gadolinium is usually not added during pregnancy, this contrast may enhance images .

579
01:15:09,567 --> 01:15:25,579
Lax and coworkers (2007) described three MR imaging findings that suggest PAS: uterine bulging, dark intraplacental bands on T2-weighted imaging, and heterogeneous signal intensity within the placenta indicative of lacunae.

580
01:15:25,579 --> 01:15:38,942
Some recommend MR imaging if sonography results are inconclusive or there is a posterior previa (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2018; Silver, 2018).

581
01:15:38,942 --> 01:15:41,167
# Management.

582
01:15:41,167 --> 01:15:47,417
Preoperative assessment ideally begins once a possible PAS is recognized antenatally.

583
01:15:47,417 --> 01:15:52,504
A major decision concerns the timing and the ideal facility for delivery.

584
01:15:52,504 --> 01:15:59,842
Considerations include appropriate surgical, anesthesia, intensive care, and blood banking capabilities.

585
01:15:59,842 --> 01:16:13,604
An obstetrical surgeon or gynecological oncologist and surgical, urological, and interventional radiological consultants should be available (Collins, 2019; Shamshirsaz, 2018).

586
01:16:13,604 --> 01:16:24,779
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018) and the Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine recommend planned delivery in a tertiary-care facility.

587
01:16:24,779 --> 01:16:29,579
In some, specially designed teams have been assembled and are on call .

588
01:16:29,579 --> 01:16:34,754
A metaanalysis described the improved maternal outcomes with such management .

589
01:16:34,754 --> 01:16:45,642
Silver and colleagues (2015b) have provided requisites for accreta centers of excellence and their criteria to consider transfer to a higher levelof-care hospital .

590
01:16:45,642 --> 01:16:52,142
If possible, delivery is best scheduled for peak availability of all resources and team members.

591
01:16:52,142 --> 01:16:58,942
Even so, a third of cases require unscheduled delivery, and contingency plans should be ready .

592
01:16:58,942 --> 01:17:04,717
Women who refuse blood or its derivatives pose especially difficult management dilemmas .

593
01:17:04,717 --> 01:17:07,279
Table 43-2.

594
01:17:07,279 --> 01:17:09,292
Criteria to

595
01:17:09,460 --> 01:17:23,885
Consider for Delivery of Placenta Accreta Spectrum (PAS) in a Center of Excellence Delivery Timing In one study, 40 percent of women with PAS had bleeding mandating unplanned delivery prior to 34 weeks’ gestation.

596
01:17:23,835 --> 01:17:35,535
Timing balances fetal immaturity against adverse maternal consequences of hemorrhage and emergency cesarean delivery (Perlman, 2017; Pettit, 2019).

597
01:17:35,535 --> 01:17:49,960
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018; 2021) recommends individualization and suggests delivery between 340/7 and 356/7 weeks’ gestation.

598
01:17:49,960 --> 01:17:58,485
They cite a decision-analysis study that justifies elective delivery without fetal lung maturity testing after 34 completed weeks .

599
01:17:58,485 --> 01:18:06,685
The Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine (2018) recommends delivery between 34 and 37 weeks.

600
01:18:06,685 --> 01:18:17,410
Two earlier surveys found that most practitioners do not deliver these women until 36 weeks or later (Esakoff, 2012; Wright, 2013).

601
01:18:17,410 --> 01:18:27,172
At Parkland Hospital, we generally schedule these procedures after 36 completed weeks but are prepared also to manage them in nonelective situations .

602
01:18:27,172 --> 01:18:32,060
In some cases, PAS is not recognized until laparotomy.

603
01:18:32,060 --> 01:18:40,072
If resources are inadequate to surgically manage the percreta and if the woman is stable and not bleeding, the fetus is not delivered.

604
01:18:40,072 --> 01:18:45,597
The abdominal incision is closed, and she is transferred to a tertiary-care facility.

605
01:18:45,597 --> 01:18:54,635
Preoperative Prophylactic Catheterization In cases that may involve the ureters, catheterization may aid their dissection or identification.

606
01:18:54,635 --> 01:19:00,385
However, these catheters overall do not lower urinary tract injury rates .

607
01:19:00,385 --> 01:19:11,585
Some, but not all, advocate preoperative ureteral catheterization (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2018; Eller, 2011).

608
01:19:11,585 --> 01:19:18,610
The role of catheters for ureteral injury repair is outlined in Chapter 30 (p. 564).

609
01:19:18,610 --> 01:19:26,460
With arterial catheterization, balloon-tipped catheters aim to mitigate blood loss and thereby enhance surgical visibility.

610
01:19:26,460 --> 01:19:40,847
Catheters are advanced preoperatively into the internal iliac arteries and are inflated after fetal delivery to occlude pelvic blood flow (Ballas, 2012; Desai, 2012; Zhou, 2021).

611
01:19:40,847 --> 01:19:45,185
Some prefer an infrarenal aortic balloon occlusion catheter .

612
01:19:45,185 --> 01:19:51,735
Alternatively, the catheters can be used to deliver occluding emboli to bleeding arterial sites .

613
01:19:51,735 --> 01:20:03,022
Others have concluded that these procedures offer borderline efficacy and have serious risks (Chen, 2020; Makary, 2019; Mohr-Sasson, 2020).

614
01:20:03,022 --> 01:20:08,072
Rare complications have included thrombosis of the common iliac artery .

615
01:20:08,072 --> 01:20:20,122
At this time, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018) concludes that a firm recommendation cannot be made for or against intraarterial catheter use.

616
01:20:20,122 --> 01:20:30,347
Similarly, there are no obvious benefits to prophylactic internal iliac artery ligation in this setting (Po, 2012; Yu, 2020).

617
01:20:30,347 --> 01:20:39,072
Cesarean Delivery and Hysterectomy Before commencing with delivery, the risk of hysterectomy to prevent exsanguination should be estimated.

618
01:20:39,072 --> 01:20:47,647
Some of these abnormal placentations, especially if partial, may be amenable to placental delivery with hemostatic suture placement.

619
01:20:47,647 --> 01:20:52,922
Confirmation of a percreta or increta almost always mandates hysterectomy.

620
01:20:52,922 --> 01:21:00,797
Because the scope of invasion may not be apparent before delivery of the fetus, some complete a number of dissection steps early.

621
01:21:00,797 --> 01:21:08,085
This also minimizes blood loss during potentially tedious dissection after hysterotomy and if the placenta bleeds.

622
01:21:08,085 --> 01:21:14,435
Thus, an attempt can be made to create a wide bladder flap before making the hysterotomy incision .

623
01:21:14,435 --> 01:21:21,122
The round ligaments are divided, and the lateral edges of the peritoneal reflection are dissected downward.

624
01:21:21,122 --> 01:21:31,435
If possible, these incisions are extended to encircle the entire placental implantation site that visibly occupies the prevesical space and posterior bladder wall.

625
01:21:31,435 --> 01:21:39,497
Following this, a classical hysterotomy, transverse fundal, or high posterior incision is made to avoid the placenta .

626
01:21:39,497 --> 01:21:46,572
After fetal delivery, the extent of placental invasion is assessed without attempts at manual placental removal.

627
01:21:46,572 --> 01:21:55,785
In a report from the United Kingdom, attempts at partial or total placental removal prior to hysterectomy were associated with twice as much blood loss .

628
01:21:55,785 --> 01:21:57,335
Generally

629
01:21:57,484 --> 01:22:05,546
speaking, with obvious percreta or increta, hysterectomy is usually the best course, and the placenta is left in situ.

630
01:22:05,496 --> 01:22:12,484
With more extensive placental ingrowth, bleeding may be minimal until manual placental removal is attempted.

631
01:22:12,484 --> 01:22:21,421
Unless there is spontaneous separation with bleeding that mandates emergency hysterectomy, the operation begins after full assessment is made.

632
01:22:21,421 --> 01:22:27,634
Cesarean hysterectomy is carried out as described in Chapter 30 (p.560).

633
01:22:27,634 --> 01:22:39,084
With heavy bleeding, successful treatment depends on immediate blood replacement and other measures that can include uterine or internal iliac artery ligation, balloon occlusion, or embolization.

634
01:22:39,084 --> 01:22:45,459
In some cases, the operation is made easier by performance of a supracervical hysterectomy .

635
01:22:45,459 --> 01:22:52,046
Kingdom and colleagues (2020) have described a modified radical hysterectomy for PAS .

636
01:22:52,046 --> 01:22:58,334
This technique is also detailed in Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd edition .

637
01:22:58,334 --> 01:23:05,984
Because of a high risk for postoperative venous thromboembolism, consideration is given for thromboprophylaxis .

638
01:23:05,984 --> 01:23:08,521
Table 43-3.

639
01:23:08,521 --> 01:23:23,621
Steps in Cesarean Hysterectomy for Management of Placenta Percreta Conservative Management Occasionally, it may be possible to trim the umbilical cord, repair the hysterotomy incision, leave the placenta in situ, and not pursue hysterectomy .

640
01:23:23,621 --> 01:23:32,746
This option may be used for women in whom abnormal placentation was not suspected before cesarean delivery and in whom uterine closure stops bleeding.

641
01:23:32,746 --> 01:23:38,959
After this, she can be transferred to a higher level-ofcare facility for definitive management.

642
01:23:38,959 --> 01:23:45,821
Another consideration is the woman with a strong desire for fertility and who has received extensive counseling.

643
01:23:45,821 --> 01:23:53,059
Resection of the adherent placenta and underlying myometrium has been advocated as a uterine-sparing technique .

644
01:23:53,059 --> 01:24:01,484
Conservative management was reviewed by Perez-Delboy (2014) and Fox (2015) and their colleagues.

645
01:24:01,484 --> 01:24:08,759
In some of these cases, the placenta spontaneously resorbed between 1 and 12 months with a mean of 6 months.

646
01:24:08,759 --> 01:24:23,146
Numerous complications can occur and include sepsis, coagulopathy, pulmonary embolism, and arteriovenous malformation (Fox, 2015; Judy, 2015; Roach, 2015).

647
01:24:23,146 --> 01:24:29,596
In other cases, sutures are placed through the outside wall of the uterus overlying the placental bed.

648
01:24:29,596 --> 01:24:35,521
Shih and associates (2019) described this using a Nausicaa procedure.

649
01:24:35,521 --> 01:24:53,134
In some of these women, a subsequent hysterectomy—either planned or prompted by bleeding or infection—is performed days to weeks postpartum when blood loss might be less (Al-Khan, 2014; Sentilhes, 2009, 2021; Zuckerwise, 2020).

650
01:24:53,134 --> 01:25:02,896
In some studies, 20 percent or fewer of women ultimately required hysterectomy (Bretelle, 2007; Kutuk, 2018).

651
01:25:02,896 --> 01:25:13,546
In other reports, however, up to 60 percent eventually required emergency hysterectomy (Clausen, 2013; Pather, 2014).

652
01:25:13,546 --> 01:25:18,084
Evidence that treatment with methotrexate aids resorption is lacking.

653
01:25:18,084 --> 01:25:33,584
Last, for women in whom the placenta is left in situ, serial serum β-hCG measurements are not informative, and serial sonographic or MR imaging is recommended (Timmermans, 2007; Worley, 2008).

654
01:25:33,584 --> 01:25:44,409
At this time, we agree with the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018) that leaving the placenta in situ should be considered investigational.

655
01:25:44,409 --> 01:25:48,309
Exceptions permit transfer to a higher level of care.

656
01:25:48,309 --> 01:25:54,534
Guidelines from the International Society for Abnormally Invasive Placenta are more permissive .

657
01:25:54,534 --> 01:25:57,271
# Pregnancy Outcomes.

658
01:25:57,271 --> 01:26:02,771
In sum, PAS can have disastrous outcomes for both mother and fetus.

659
01:26:02,771 --> 01:26:10,646
Although the depth of placental invasion does not correspond with perinatal outcome, it is of paramount maternal significance.

660
01:26:10,646 --> 01:26:19,821
Table 43-4 displays outcomes of women at tertiary-care hospitals and in whom the PAS diagnosis was usually made preoperatively.

661
01:26:19,821 --> 01:26:31,071
Despite these advantages, complications included hemorrhage, urinary tract injury, intensive care unit admission, secondary surgical procedures, and maternal death.

662
01:26:31,071 --> 01:26:43,021
Some of these same reports chronicle outcomes in a second cohort of women in whom care was not given at a tertiary-care facility or in whom the diagnosis of percreta was not made until delivery, or both.

663
01:26:43,021 --> 01:26:47,034
In these cohorts, morbidity and mortality rates

664
01:26:47,188 --> 01:26:48,788
were higher .

665
01:26:48,738 --> 01:26:51,325
Table 43-4.

666
01:26:51,325 --> 01:26:59,388
Selected Maternal Outcomes in Women with Placenta Accreta Spectrum Identified Prenatally and Delivered in Tertiary-Care Units

